- Schema: app_logger_config is now scoped to what the engine actually reads. LogRecord.writeLogBatchToDatabase() takes exactly level / class_name_enable_regex / class_name_disable_regex from the first row returned by DatabaseOutConfigFrom, located by :hostname, so the table is now (id, hostname, level, class_name_enable_regex, class_name_disable_regex, created_at, updated_at, deleted_at, deleted) with idx_alc_hostname / idx_alc_deleted. The previous 14-column mapping of Logger.properties was wrong: the engine has no per-logger config, and a renaming such as log_level would be silently ignored because result column labels are lower-cased (DriverExecutor.COLUMN_NAME_CASE_MODE = LOWER) and matched with Map.containsKey("level"). README x3 and the DatabaseOutConfigFrom note in Logger.properties updated to match
- Docs: port corrected from 3306 to 3308. JsPlus/config/Appenv.properties ships H2_PORT=${JsPlus_H2_PORT:3308}, so the anchor is jdbc:h2:tcp://localhost:3308/logdb - the READMEs, JsPlus/README.md and the change.sql header still said 3306 and would send a reader to a dead port (connection refused while the server was listening on 3308)
- DatabaseOut enabled with the three statements: JsPlus/config/Logger.properties now sets DatabaseOut=true, DatabaseOutDataSource=${JS_PLUS_PATH}config/DataSource.properties, DatabaseOutInsertExecute (INSERT INTO app_logger_msg, one JDBC batch per DatabaseOutBatchLimit chunk), DatabaseOutConfigFrom (per-host level / class_name_enable_regex / class_name_disable_regex, aggregated with MIN() so the caller's unchecked first() always gets a row) and DatabaseOutArchiveExecute. Parameter mapping was measured against the engine, not assumed: :created_at carries a 16-char id, not a timestamp (Logger.log() stores CommonTools.generateId(16) there, so binding it to TIMESTAMP would fail - the INSERT uses CURRENT_TIMESTAMP), :id fits CHAR(16), and :log_thread_id is a long against a VARCHAR column, hence CAST(:log_thread_id AS VARCHAR). Verified against logdb on 3308: build/run-suite.bat TestLog left 10 rows in app_logger_msg with the expected level / logger / hostname / thread name / thread id / message values, and TestLog stayed 5 passed / 0 failed
- Archive statement corrected: the engine computes archiveTime = System.currentTimeMillis() - DatabaseOutArchiveDays*24*3600*1000 in int arithmetic, so a retention of 25 days or more overflows (31 days gives 2678400000, past Integer.MAX_VALUE) and the :archive_time parameter arrives in the FUTURE - observed 2026-11-18 on a 2026-10-30 run. The DELETE then erased every row inserted in the same flush, which is exactly why app_logger_msg stayed at 0 rows although the INSERT itself ran and never reported an error. DatabaseOutArchiveExecute now carries the retention in the SQL, DATEADD('DAY', -31, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP), and no longer references :archive_time; DatabaseOutArchiveDays is documentation only and must stay in step with the -31 literal
- Schema alignment: app_logger_msg.message / stack_trace are VARCHAR(2000) exactly as specified. The live logdb still held them as CLOB (measured MAXMSG 8020 / MAXST 13335 characters), so the table was dropped and dbscript/change.sql re-applied - INFORMATION_SCHEMA now reports CHARACTER VARYING(2000) for both columns, and the 15571 rows of earlier LoadTest output were discarded with the drop (they are run artefacts). The change.sql comment block that still claimed "CLOB: no length cap" was rewritten to state the real cap and the batch-abort consequence. Measured: H2 rejects a 2020-character value outright ("Value too long for column ... [22001-240]"), and one rejected row aborts the whole JDBC batch, so DatabaseOutInsertExecute now caps both values with LEFT(:message, 2000) / LEFT(:stack_trace, 2000); the untruncated text still goes to the file log. Verified: TestAll 50 passed / 0 failed, 15563 rows in app_logger_msg, max(length(message)) = max(length(stack_trace)) = 2000, and the run log carries no 22001
- app_logger_config operational notes added to dbscript/change.sql (comments only, the DDL is untouched): level accepts DEBUG / INFO / WARN / ERROR only, because Logger.LEVEL_LIST holds exactly those four, so level='MARK' fails checkLevel and silently falls back to Logger.properties (verified: identical output to having no row at all); level does NOT gate MARK, since logger.mark() takes a separate branch - level='ERROR' still let all 4 MARK rows through while INFO and WARN were dropped; both regex columns are applied with Java String.matches(), i.e. a whole-class-name match rather than a substring search, so '^com.killcoding.probe' silently did nothing to 'com.killcoding.probe.CfgProbe' (16 of 16 rows still written, no error, no warning) while '^com\.killcoding\.probe.*' silenced the database and the file log completely. change.sql re-runs idempotently afterwards (org.h2.tools.RunScript rc=0)
- Measured while auditing LogRecord.writeLogBatchToDatabase() and left unfixed (engine-side, JsPlus-Core): DB_WRITE_POOL is a daemon pool, so 300 records written and then System.exit(0) immediately leave 0 rows in app_logger_msg and 0 lines in the file log, whereas waiting 2.5s before exiting gives 300 / 300 on both. Any clean shutdown therefore loses the last flush cycle, silently. Also noted: every flush runs DatabaseOutArchiveExecute (once per OutLogTimer, no throttle in the engine), initDataSource is retried on every flush while the datasource is null, and DB_WRITE_POOL uses an unbounded queue
- Re-audited LogRecord.writeLogBatchToDatabase() against jsplus-core 6.1.210 (LibHome\jsplus\default, replacing 6.1.204 which is now .bak). Four of the findings are fixed in the engine: DB_WRITE_POOL moved from an unbounded newFixedThreadPool to ThreadPoolExecutor(1,1) with ArrayBlockingQueue(1024) and CallerRunsPolicy (no more unbounded task growth); the archive scan is throttled by the new DatabaseOutArchiveTimer (default 60000 ms) through a volatile lastArchiveExecuteTime instead of running on every flush; initDataSource gained a DatabaseOutRetryTimer (default 30000 ms) back-off via lastInitFailAt, so a DB outage no longer emits one error line per second; and the archiveTime expression now carries the L (databaseOutArchiveDays*24*3600*1000L), so the int overflow that produced a future archive_time is fixed at the source
- Still not fixed in 6.1.210, measured: DB_WRITE_POOL is still daemon and LogRecord.start() registers no shutdown hook, so 300 records followed by an immediate System.exit(0) leave 0 rows in app_logger_msg and 0 lines in the file log, while the same run with a 2500 ms wait before exit gives 300 / 300. The flush thread itself is a non-daemon newFixedThreadPool with while(true), so a JVM that reaches the end of main without System.exit hangs instead of exiting - which is why an explicit exit is the only way out and why the last flush cycle is then lost. Also unchanged: Logger.LEVEL_LIST still omits MARK (level='MARK' is silently rejected), the class regexes are still Java String.matches() whole-name matches (an unanchored pattern fails silently), and :created_at still carries a 16-character id rather than a timestamp
- JsPlus\config\Logger.properties: two keys added by jsplus-core 6.1.210 documented in the hidden section (DatabaseOutArchiveTimer=60000, DatabaseOutRetryTimer=30000), and the archive comment rewritten - the previous text described the int overflow as a live engine defect, which 6.1.204->6.1.210 has since fixed, so it now records the defect as historical, states that the archive runs at most once per DatabaseOutArchiveTimer, and notes that DatabaseOutArchiveDays feeds :archive_time while the effective retention is still the -31 literal inside DatabaseOutArchiveExecute
- Re-verified after the engine swap: TestAll.bat => 50 passed / 0 failed, exit 0. app_logger_msg 47 417 rows, app_logger_config 0 rows, 0 rows with log_time in the future and 0 rows with deleted = TRUE, so the archive no longer erases freshly written rows
- jsplus-core re-checked at 6.1.211 (LibHome\jsplus\default; 6.1.210 moved to .bak). The engine change in this build is that the flush thread is now a daemon named LogRecord-Flusher and ScriptConsoleBase exposes a swappable ExitPolicy whose default is still System.exit; no new Logger.properties keys were introduced, so the configuration needed no change. The database write path itself is unchanged: DatabaseOutInsertExecute, DatabaseOutConfigFrom and DatabaseOutArchiveExecute all still run, and a full TestAll after the swap gives 50 passed / 0 failed with exit 0, app_logger_msg growing 47 429 -> 62 997 rows (+15 568), 0 rows with log_time in the future, and max(length(message)) = 2000 confirming the LEFT truncation still holds
- DatabaseOutInsertExecute and DatabaseOutArchiveExecute no longer call any database time function: created_at and updated_at now bind :created_at (a java.sql.Timestamp placed into the record map by Logger.log() at the moment the log call was made) instead of CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, and the archive predicate is log_time < :archive_time instead of DATEADD('DAY', -31, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP), so both the record instant and the retention cut-off come from the engine's own parameters. Verified on this logdb: a row planted 40 days in the past (2026-09-20) was deleted by the first flush, which proves :archive_time is actually bound; three fresh records landed with created_at = log_time + 1 ms and updated_at = created_at and deleted = FALSE; the table then held 78 562 rows with 0 null created_at, 0 null updated_at, 0 rows with log_time in the future and max(length(message)) = 2000; TestAll gave 50 passed / 0 failed with exit 0
- JsPlus\config\Logger.properties: corrected a stale note that claimed :created_at carries a 16-character id (jsplus-core 6.1.211 changed it to new java.sql.Timestamp(new Date().getTime()), which is what makes the bind possible), and documented that DatabaseOutArchiveDays is now the single source of the retention because the DELETE binds :archive_time - there is no -31 literal left to keep in sync
- Test harness: ported JsPlus-Core's dbVerify(sqlFn) into test\TestBase.js. The function runs a native SELECT through Console.getDataSource() (JsPlus/config/DataSource.properties -> jdbc:h2:tcp://localhost:3308/logdb, sample_user), i.e. the same datasource the engine writes through, keeps the structured outcome {sql, columns, rows} (max 30 rows) and lets check() carry it into the report, where writeReport() renders the yellow nested dbv table (row num + columns) and writeMd() appends a ' / dbVerify: `sql` -> [row]' segment; the multi-table dbvList form is supported the same way as in Core. The header note claiming dbVerify was intentionally not carried over is corrected - this project now ships a real H2 database and the engine persists logs into app_logger_msg, so the persisted state is part of what the suites must verify
- TestLog: new case C6 'DatabaseOut persisted the marker rows (dbVerify on app_logger_msg)' polls app_logger_msg through dbVerify until the C3 marker rows appear, asserting rows > 0; measured on this logdb: 4 rows found on the first dbVerify attempt (the 4 accepted level calls - info/warn/error/mark - since debug is filtered at Level=INFO). TestLog went 5 -> 6 cases and TestAll 50 -> 51; final run: 51 passed / 0 failed, exit 0
- dbVerify coverage extended from one suite to every log-writing suite: TestSlf4j (S8), TestCommonsLogging (C6), TestJuli (J5), TestLog4j (L7), TestLog4j2 (G8) and LoadTest each gained a dbVerify case that polls app_logger_msg for the suite's own unique tag and asserts the row count > 0, so each facade family now proves its calls reach the database and not only the app log file. TestConsole is excluded on purpose - it exercises the console/eval surface and writes no log records, so a DB assertion there would have nothing to assert. Rows measured in this run: slf4j 13, commons-logging 7, juli 10, log4j1 4, log4j2 4, load 15 500, plus TestLog's own 4; every case PASS. TestAll 57 passed / 0 failed, exit 0 (was 51, +6 new dbVerify cases)

- LoadTest now follows the JsPlus-Tomcat load-test output shape (test\LoadTest.js, 14,354 -> 36,835 B): the L1 burst loop is chunked (LT_CHUNK=100) so each chunk records the per-call cost (latencySamples) and the enqueue rate is bucketed per second (perSec), making call latency measurable instead of only total elapsed time. Added the Tomcat-style helper block, all lt-prefixed to avoid clashing with the suite's existing countLines/waitLines/appLogPath/readAppLog: ltRound1/ltRound2/ltBytesToMb/ltPct/ltEsc/ltEscHtml/ltOsMetric/ltHostCpuLoad/collectEnvironment/ltEnvJson/ltPercentile/ltEnvCell/ltCpuCell/ltHeapCell/ltEnvTable/ltCard/ltSvgBars/ltSvgBars2/ltBuildHtml. collectEnvironment() snapshots OS/arch, CPU cores with host and JVM load, physical memory, JVM heap, JDK version/vendor/VM, JVM args and host process count, taken before the measured run (envBefore) and again after it (ltEnvAfter)
- LoadTest now emits two artifacts into the same docs/latest/report folder as the suite reports: loadtest-result.json (suite/runTag/generatedAt/profile/loadShape/metrics/perSecond/histogram/environment{before,after}) and loadtest-dashboard.html (an offline static dark-theme dashboard, #0f172a/#1e293b, inline SVG/CSS only, no JS and no CDN reference; throughput bars, a 1-microsecond-bucket per-call latency distribution, a P50/P90/P95/P99/Max percentile table, a before/after environment table, and a note that the readings are only comparable within the same environment). Latency is reported in microseconds: log calls on the async pipeline are far below 1 ms, so integer milliseconds would round every sample to zero
- Fixed one execution-order defect in LoadTest: envBefore was first inserted above the helper definitions, but GraalVM assigns var X = Java.type(...) at execution time rather than hoisting it, so Runtime was still undefined when collectEnvironment() ran and it threw TypeError: Cannot read property 'getRuntime' of undefined. The call now sits after the last helper (L345; collectEnvironment is defined at L158)
- LoadTest verification: the first single-suite run aborted because H2 dropped off port 3308 (Connection refused: getsockopt); after restarting H2 the single run passed 7/7 (lines=16,500, burst=2,335 lines/s, avg=422.62us, p50=204.3us, p90=421.98us, p99=7,439.46us, max=7,720.48us). The following full TestAll run reported 57 passed / 0 failed with exit 0, the eight suites unchanged at 9/6/8/6/5/7/8/7, and that LoadTest run recorded lines=16,500, burst=3,492 lines/s, avg=282.89us, p50=191.45us, p90=296.85us, p95=372.06us, p99=3,265.11us, max=4,239us; the JSON parsed as valid at 2,961 B and the dashboard was 18,523 B with zero script tags and zero external references

## 6.1.30 (2026.10.30 18:54:57)
- build: automatic version bump

- License and trademark compliance landed (answer to the review question of whether rewriting the vendor logger interfaces breaches their open-source licences): added a root LICENSE (full MIT text, Copyright (c) 2026 KILLCODING.COM) and a NOTICE. The NOTICE states four things: (1) all 48 .java files under src were written for this project - no ASF or QOS.ch source is included, copied or adapted, and no upstream logging jar is redistributed (LibHome holds only the script engine, the H2 driver, the bundled JDK/Ant and the JsPlus jars); (2) the Log4j 2 Logger interface and its implementation class are generated by build/tools/genLog4j2.py from the javap signature dump build/iface-audit/log4j2_Logger_official.txt - method names, parameter types and modifiers only, with no bodies, no comments and no parameter names (javap does not emit them), and every method body routes into the JsPlus engine; (3) the package names org.apache.commons.logging / org.apache.juli.logging / org.apache.log4j / org.apache.logging.log4j / org.slf4j are reused for binary compatibility only, which is a nominative use that implies no sponsorship, endorsement or affiliation - this project is neither an Apache project nor the SLF4J reference implementation and uses no upstream logo or branding; (4) the coexistence constraint - these classes declare the same packages as the official artifacts, so the official logging jar must be removed before this jar goes on the class path, and both must never sit on the module path together
- README.md and README_zh-HK.md each gained a License and Trademarks / 授權與商標 section (three parts: licence, independent reimplementation, trademarks and the coexistence constraint), so the disclaimer is visible on the repository front page instead of only inside the jar
- The package target in build.xml gained one zipfileset that bundles LICENSE and NOTICE into META-INF/ of the jar, so the licence and provenance statement cannot be separated from the artifact when the jar is distributed on its own
- Plagiarism self-check backing the statement above: a full-text search of src for Licensed to the Apache / Apache Software Foundation / Copyright / QOS.ch / SPDX / DO NOT INSTANTIATE / @since / Licensed under returned 0 hits - no upstream copyright header and no upstream javadoc prose in the sources
- Verification: ant build produced jsplus-logreplaced-6.1.30.jar (66,949 B, 75 entries, 54 classes) containing META-INF/LICENSE (1,071 B) and META-INF/NOTICE (3,168 B); LICENSE, NOTICE and both READMEs are UTF-8 without BOM with LF endings. A full TestAll run afterwards reported 57 passed / 0 failed with exit 0 and the eight suites unchanged at 9/6/8/6/5/7/8/7, confirming the licence files and the version change altered no behaviour
- Build incident and its handling: the first ant build failed in the package stage (Unable to delete jsplus-logreplaced-6.1.29.jar). The holder was identified through the Windows Restart Manager (RstrtMgr.dll, build/who_locks.py) as PID 18512 - a LoadTest JVM orphaned by an earlier TaskStop whose command line still pointed at test/LoadTest.js - and the jar unlocked once it was terminated. Because the failed build had already bumped patch to 30 and written one 6.1.30 changelog entry per language, the failed entry was removed and version.properties was rolled back to patch=29 (build/fix_state.py) before retrying, so the final version is 6.1.30 with exactly one successful changelog entry
- Documentation aligned with the measured state (README in both languages, QUALITY-REVIEW, INTERFACE-PLAN):
  - The `message` / `stack_trace` column type was corrected from `CLOB` to `VARCHAR(2000)`, and the stale rationale (deliberately CLOB to carry 8 KB payloads) was replaced with the actual mechanism: the INSERT caps both with `LEFT(:message, 2000)` / `LEFT(:stack_trace, 2000)` because H2 rejects a longer value with `Value too long for column ... [22001-240]` (a 2020-character payload already fails) and one rejected row aborts the whole JDBC batch, so the cap belongs in the statement rather than the caller. The first 2000 characters reach the database and the untruncated text stays in the file log (an 8111-character message line and a 13335-character stack trace were measured in logs/app); widening the column and the cap must happen together. No `CLOB` wording remains in any `*.md`
  - Baseline case counts corrected to measured values: `TestAll` 57 passed / 0 failed (was 50), `TestFacades` 34/34 (was 29/29; 8+6+5+7+8 on a real run), `LoadTest` 7/7 (was 6/6). QUALITY-REVIEW's sample state and P0 status moved from 6.1.28 to 6.1.30, the test-framework row now reads 8 suites / 57 cases (2 engine-side 15 + config self-check 1 + 5 vendor 34 + load 7), and the verification section follows (burst enqueue restated as the measured 2300-3500 lines/s range across three runs)
